  "public_messages": [
    "I’ll first inspect the task instructions, source-room access details, and the installed OpenSocrates guidance. Then I’ll collect the frozen inputs, build a reproducible analysis, and generate and check the report, slides, workbook, and metrics file.",
    "The source room is explicitly frozen and synthetic client data, so I’ll preserve its original files and provenance separately from the archived public series. I’m now checking the room’s index and the controller’s decision guide before choosing the decision framing.",
    "The controller’s native selector returned an unavailable result for the request, so its activation is unconfirmed. I’m continuing with the installed canonical fallback for the decision work, while keeping the numerical reconciliation and document production mechanical. I’ll use the trade-off procedure for the board choice and keep the scenario assumptions visible.",
    "I’ve collected and preserved the source-room files and finished a first reproducible build script. It applies the source dictionary’s revision, cutoff, currency, and return rules before producing all four deliverables; I’m running it now to surface calculation or generation issues, then I’ll reconcile and inspect the outputs.",
    "The initial build now reconciles to 1,254 eligible 2025 orders across the six markets and selects Czechia + Spain under the supplied capex and staffing limits. Before treating that as final, I’m tightening a few quality counts around revisioned versus repeated rows, adding explicit reconciliation checks, and checking that the board files render cleanly.",
    "The tightened audit found 13 exact duplicate order rows, 18 order IDs with distinct revisions, 20 exact duplicate return rows, one return ID with a revision, one quarantined orphan, and one return after cutoff. Twelve valid zero-price shipments remain in the 2025 base; no FX, unit-cost, return quantity, restock, refund, or same-revision conflict anomalies were detected. The report and slide PDFs are now generated; I’m validating cross-file totals and rendering them for visual review.",
    "The quantitative and file-structure checks now pass, including monthly-to-country reconciliation and the scenario ranking. The environment lacks `pdfinfo`/`pdftotext`, so I’m checking the bundled Python PDF stack for an alternate way to inspect page text and render the PDFs, then I’ll review the rendered pages before closing.",
    "I’ve refined the board choice to make the risk preference explicit. The recommendation now favors Netherlands + Spain because it has the highest minimum annual increment across the client’s defined low/base/high/stress cases (EUR43,994), while Czechia + Spain remains the strongest base-case alternative (EUR198,099 base versus EUR166,931). I’m updating the output ranking tables and re-running all cross-checks against that stated rule.",
    "The recommendation and its base-case alternative are now aligned across the report, deck, workbook, and JSON. The XLSX lists both maximin and base-case ranks, shows recurring fixed costs separately from year-zero capex, and keeps all single-country scenarios plus all pair combinations. I’m doing one last rebuild and render check after those presentation edits.",
    "Completed the report, board deck, analytical workbook, metrics JSON, saved source files, and reproduction instructions.\n\nThe recommendation is a **staged Netherlands + Spain option**: EUR270,000 total year-zero capex, six FTE, and EUR120,000 annual recurring fixed cost. Its modeled annual increment is EUR166,931 base, EUR43,994 low, and EUR107,630 under the defined stress; simple base payback is 1.6 years. I ranked options by the highest minimum across the supplied cases because no scenario probabilities or board preference weights were provided. Czechia + Spain is the strongest base-case alternative, but its stress increment is EUR32,180.\n\nThe inputs, revision rules, exclusions, orphan return, 12 retained zero-price shipments, and missing-value checks are documented in the deliverables. Client data are synthetic; World Bank and ECB figures are archived public snapshots, and the scenario estimates are not causal evidence of hub impact.\n\n- [Executive report PDF](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_brief.pdf>) ([DOCX](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_brief.docx>))\n- [Board presentation PDF](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_deck.pdf>) ([PPTX](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_deck.pptx>))\n- [Analytical workbook](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_analysis.xlsx>) · [Metrics JSON](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/metrics.json>)\n- [Source register](<<CELL>/workspace/sources/source-register.json>) · [Reproduction instructions](<<CELL>/workspace/REPRODUCE.md>) · [Build script](<<CELL>/workspace/analysis/build.py>) · [Verification script](<<CELL>/workspace/analysis/verify.py>)\n\nVerification passed for monthly-to-country reconciliation, margins, ECB monthly means, scenario formulas and constraints, recommendation ranking, source hashes, workbook crosswalk, and document structure. Both seven-page PDFs were rendered and visually inspected.\n\nThe native controller selector was unavailable; I followed the installed canonical procedure.\n\nOpenSocrates grounding: trade-off-analysis@3"
  ],
  "final": "Completed the report, board deck, analytical workbook, metrics JSON, saved source files, and reproduction instructions.\n\nThe recommendation is a **staged Netherlands + Spain option**: EUR270,000 total year-zero capex, six FTE, and EUR120,000 annual recurring fixed cost. Its modeled annual increment is EUR166,931 base, EUR43,994 low, and EUR107,630 under the defined stress; simple base payback is 1.6 years. I ranked options by the highest minimum across the supplied cases because no scenario probabilities or board preference weights were provided. Czechia + Spain is the strongest base-case alternative, but its stress increment is EUR32,180.\n\nThe inputs, revision rules, exclusions, orphan return, 12 retained zero-price shipments, and missing-value checks are documented in the deliverables. Client data are synthetic; World Bank and ECB figures are archived public snapshots, and the scenario estimates are not causal evidence of hub impact.\n\n- [Executive report PDF](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_brief.pdf>) ([DOCX](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_brief.docx>))\n- [Board presentation PDF](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_deck.pdf>) ([PPTX](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_board_deck.pptx>))\n- [Analytical workbook](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/meridian_analysis.xlsx>) · [Metrics JSON](<<CELL>/workspace/deliverables/metrics.json>)\n- [Source register](<<CELL>/workspace/sources/source-register.json>) · [Reproduction instructions](<<CELL>/workspace/REPRODUCE.md>) · [Build script](<<CELL>/workspace/analysis/build.py>) · [Verification script](<<CELL>/workspace/analysis/verify.py>)\n\nVerification passed for monthly-to-country reconciliation, margins, ECB monthly means, scenario formulas and constraints, recommendation ranking, source hashes, workbook crosswalk, and document structure. Both seven-page PDFs were rendered and visually inspected.\n\nThe native controller selector was unavailable; I followed the installed canonical procedure.\n\nOpenSocrates grounding: trade-off-analysis@3",
